WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.650
The man sprinted through the hallways of Site-01
as fast as he could.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.480
Bullets whizzed over his shoulders as panicked
shouts went up all around him.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.400
The whole facility was bathed in red as the
security alarms deafened his ears.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.410
He had to move fast, had to get out of here
before it was too late.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:22.230
A force smacked him in the back so hard that
he fell onto his face, skidding across the

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.539
vinyl floor.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:27.280
Planting both hands on the ground and trying
to push himself back up, he felt his right

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.590
shoulder blade creaking and bending against
itself.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:35.570
He'd been shot enough times in his life to
know what a bullet-shattered bone felt like.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.550
Trying his best to ignore the pain washing
through his body, he kept running.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:40.780
More bullets hit him.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.199
In his torso, his shoulders, his arms, and
his neck.

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:48.990
One even caught the top of his skull, slicing
a clear line through his hair, but he kept

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:49.990
on running.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:54.140
The doors were slowly closing in front of
him as the Foundation personnel swarmed the

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.140
hallways.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.079
All he had to do was make it to those doors,
slide through the gap, and he'd be free.

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:02.039
If only they could understand.

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:06.500
If only all the agents, researchers, and the
Overseer Council could make sense of what

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.500
he was telling them.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.810
They would let him go through those doors
instead of trying to shoot him down like a

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:12.810
dog.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.939
Just 10 feet to go, he was nearly there, when
a bullet ripped through his calf and sent

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:18.700
him tumbling to the ground again.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.770
But this time, before he could get up, a rain
of bullets washed through his body, igniting

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:27.990
a pain so fierce that he wished it could all
just be over.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.720
But it never was.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.950
The pile of pitiful remains was wheeled back
into a containment cell with the door locked

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:34.990
behind it.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:40.220
You would think the body lying on the floor
was some kind of roadkill, but it was a man.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:46.409
A man whose body slowly and painfully, over
the course of four months, pushed every one

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:53.060
of the 247 bullets out of itself and got to
work stitching itself back together, as he

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.380
lay there weeping on his own.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.380
*

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.960
Andrea Twain had been headhunted by the Foundation
while she was still in college.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.549
Sitting in a Harvard library, she'd been hard
at work on her thesis, pouring hour after

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:07.470
hour into researching jellyfish.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:11.790
She was starting to see them in her sleep,
and she was falling asleep at her desk quite

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.790
a lot.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:17.340
Fueled by energy drinks and cups of coffee,
it was starting to seem like the jellyfish

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.640
were floating all around her between the bookshelves.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.950
That's why when she was approached by Foundation
operatives offering her a place studying the

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:29.519
most top-secret entity on the planet, she
was pretty sure it was all in her head.

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:34.110
But then, six months later, and living in
a facility that she didn't even know the location

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.269
of, it started to dawn on her the gravity
of the task she had been handed.

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:44.000
Her research topic had been the hydrozoan
Turritopsis dohrnii, a small jellyfish with

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.180
the power to reverse the aging process, dubbed
the Immortal Jellyfish.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.379
She had been right on the verge of cracking
what it was about this creature's genetic

00:02:52.379 --> 00:02:57.379
makeup that made it able to produce cells
younger than those they were replacing, a

00:02:57.379 --> 00:03:02.310
feat that could cure all number of human diseases
and revolutionize medicine and society as

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.310
we know it.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:09.599
But standing at the window in Site-01 and
looking through the glass at SCP-001, Andrea

00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:15.060
realized that all of her research had barely
scratched the surface of what was possible.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.690
On the other side of the window sat SCP-001.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.950
To many, this SCP was probably very disappointing
to look at.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:27.700
It wasn't a giant reptile, it couldn't teleport
around the facility, it didn't have any visible

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.980
powers, or really anything to distinguish
it from any of the researchers sitting on

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.230
the other side of the glass.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.500
It looked entirely like a regular human male.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:41.040
It was housed in a standard humanoid containment
cell with no added security features.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.230
It was sustained on a standard humanoid meal
program, three square meals a day with the

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.269
correct number of calories, nutrients, and
vitamins.

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:55.610
If anything, SCP-001 probably had a more balanced
diet than Andrea herself did.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.760
Some researchers have questioned the need
for feeding this SCP.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.040
It was immortal, totally unkillable, and resistant
to aging.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.480
It had been burned alive, drowned in acid,
and beaten to a pulp, and yet very slowly,

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.180
it would always recover.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.349
So why did they feed it?

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:14.260
If they didn't, it would get quite grumpy.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:18.570
More than anything in the world, Andrea was
desperate to talk to it.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.979
Containment procedures were very strict around
this SCP.

00:04:20.979 --> 00:04:26.840
There was to be no contact beyond the delivery
of meals, no conversations, no sign language,

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:27.840
nothing.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.080
It would sit in itself alone in silence, and
three times a day, a meal would be placed

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.669
in front of it by a researcher who would promptly
leave.

00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:38.360
The reason?

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.710
SCP-001 could predict the future.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:46.470
Initial research into this SCP found that
it had an uncanny ability to describe events

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:50.870
that had not yet occurred, despite having
no exposure to the outside world.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:55.259
It could predict an entire season of Major
League Baseball, right down to the individual

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:56.920
player performances.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:01.960
It foretold the fall of the Berlin Wall, the
Chernobyl meltdown, 9/11, the crash of the

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.790
housing market, and countless other events.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:11.199
In 2004, a careless researcher asked SCP-001
what the weather was looking like.

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:17.560
SCP-001 proceeded in detail to describe Hurricane
Katrina, from its formation through to the

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:24.600
humanitarian response and the political fallout,
a full year before any of those events unfolded.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.480
The Foundation has tried and failed to use
these future predictions in order to prevent

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.270
tragedies from occurring.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:35.069
But due to ironic twists of fate, any attempts
to prevent these predictions from coming true

00:05:35.069 --> 00:05:37.560
often wind up fulfilling them.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.979
In the case of Chernobyl, agents who were
deployed into the Soviet Union to work in

00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:47.300
the power plant itself wound up making critical
blunders that led to the reactor's meltdown.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.980
If the Foundation had not gotten involved,
the accident would have been averted.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:56.530
That said, taking a passive approach does
not appear to be an effective countermeasure

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:57.530
either.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:03.470
It seems that whatever SCP-001 predicts, that
course of history becomes inevitable.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:06.550
How could you not want to talk to such an
entity?

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:11.169
As Andrea stood at the glass watching it,
she knew she had to hatch a plan.

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:15.380
The Foundation may not be able to prevent
these tragedies from occurring, but surely

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.310
they should want to know what's going to happen.

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:22.990
They were researchers, after all, and their
job was to discover information.

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.150
Predicting the future?

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.009
That was the most valuable information of
all.

00:06:27.009 --> 00:06:28.490
But it went deeper than that.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.900
If anything, the predictions of the future
were a byproduct in Andrea's mind.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.930
There was something suspicious going on with
this SCP, something that the Foundation was

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.979
trying its best to keep under wraps.

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:44.810
She had seen it in the classified documents,
files left out on desks that should have been

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.060
locked away securely.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.380
Security and procedure had grown lax with
the monotony of containing this SCP.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:57.669
In these files were interview snippets, a
chance for this SCP to reveal who it truly

00:06:57.669 --> 00:06:58.669
was.

00:06:58.669 --> 00:06:59.669
*

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:02.770
At the start of the first interview, Dr. Lynch
simply pleasantly greeted SCP-001.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:07.550
He replied: “Your daughter will be hit by
a bus before her 7th birthday.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.120
I can tell you the specific day if you like.”

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:14.150
The interview session was paused as Dr. Lynch
grew distressed and had to leave his post

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.490
to go and spend time with his family.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:21.250
The interview resumed under Dr. Goldstein,
who asked SCP-001 why he felt the need to

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:24.199
give such distressing information to Dr. Lynch.

00:07:24.199 --> 00:07:29.690
He replied: “I have told you people countless
times before, you are not to refer to me as

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:30.690
SCP-001.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:36.440
I am the Founder of this organization, and
you will address me as such.”

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:41.380
When Dr Goldsteined questioned that assertion,
SCP-001 replied:

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.169
“You really don't believe everything they
have on file, do you?

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:49.930
Address me as Founder, or I will tell you
about what your husband is up to, Dr. Goldstein.”

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:52.800
Things collapsed very quickly after that.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:53.800
*

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:57.810
Andrea's plan started, as so many great plans
do, with her sprinkling a chemical compound

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.889
over one of her colleague's Cheerios.

00:07:59.889 --> 00:08:04.849
It was nothing poisonous, well, at least not
that poisonous, but it would put him out of

00:08:04.849 --> 00:08:06.710
action for a few months.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:09.949
Long enough for her to assume his position.

00:08:09.949 --> 00:08:14.210
Andrea had been next in line for a while to
take over the role of meal deliveries for

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:20.180
SCP-001, and as her fellow researcher violently
vomited into a bucket in the corner of their

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.740
office, she knew that her time had come.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:27.810
With the plastic tray in her hands, loaded
with some kind of beige slop and an apple,

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:32.570
she waited in the doorway, ready for the seals
to hiss and the door to slide slowly open

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:33.570
for her.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.630
She stepped into the room, trembling.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:40.050
She had expected the air in here to be stale,
breathed and re-breathed for decades by the

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:41.360
same man.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.420
But of course, it had been expertly filtered
and purified by the Foundation systems.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:51.459
The air in here was as clean and balanced
as it had been on the other side of the glass.

00:08:51.459 --> 00:08:54.090
The Founder looked up at her as she walked
towards him.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:59.529
She would be a new face to him, something
he hadn't seen for the prior five years.

00:08:59.529 --> 00:09:02.960
His eyes dug into her, showing no surprise
at all.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:07.149
She suddenly realized that, of course, he
wouldn't be shocked to see someone new.

00:09:07.149 --> 00:09:08.840
He could see the future.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.690
He knew that she was coming.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:14.880
Suddenly unsure of herself, she wondered what
else he knew about her future, about her most

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:19.089
closely guarded secrets, about whether her
plan would work at all.

00:09:19.089 --> 00:09:23.560
Andrea placed the meal in front of him silently,
knowing that the cameras and microphones were

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:25.709
all paying close attention to her.

00:09:25.709 --> 00:09:31.529
Subtly as she could, she nodded at SCP-001,
turned around, and left the room.

00:09:31.529 --> 00:09:35.610
Andrea's heart hammered as she sat back down
on the other side of the glass with her colleagues.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.710
Her entire plan hinged on one thing: monotony.

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:44.070
All of the checks, all of the vigilance, all
of the scrutiny that had been poured into

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:48.579
SCP-001 for decades had to have faded by now.

00:09:48.579 --> 00:09:53.240
Faded just enough for the person watching
the security cameras not to notice one word

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:59.779
etched into the plastic tray as SCP-001 took
its spoonful of food.

00:09:59.779 --> 00:10:00.779
'NOW'

00:10:00.779 --> 00:10:04.920
SCP-001 launched from its chair and sprinted
towards the door in the corner of the room.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:09.270
Before any of the other researchers could
react, Andrea slammed a hand on the open door

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:10.270
button.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:14.130
There was a hissing sound, and the SCP was
free from its containment cell.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:17.910
Confused voices filled the room, yelling over
one another in utter confusion.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:22.460
They had all been so caught off guard that
none of them had paid attention to what had

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:23.860
just happened.

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:28.330
Before they had time to put the pieces together,
Andrea was out of the door herself, running

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:29.810
down the corridor.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:32.310
There was just one door between her and the
SCP.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:36.820
She ran over to key in the security code to
open the door and let the SCP out.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:41.260
But before she could even touch the keypad,
a bullet caught her in the chest, and she

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:43.130
collapsed to the ground, wheezing.

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.740
A scared security agent stared at her wide-eyed.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:48.649
"What have you done?" he exclaimed.

00:10:48.649 --> 00:10:52.639
Then, both of them turned to look at the locked
door, waiting.

00:10:52.639 --> 00:10:53.639
*

00:10:53.639 --> 00:10:57.800
Andrea Twain had not wanted to let the SCP
out until she saw one specific line from an

00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:59.610
interview tape.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:05.399
SCP-001, referring to itself as the Founder,
explained in patient detail how it was entirely

00:11:05.399 --> 00:11:09.380
supportive of the Foundation's goals, motivations,
and actions.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.440
It had no intention of intervening with any
of their work.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:16.920
It explained that all of their ideals were,
in fact, its own, as it was the one who had

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.920
created the Foundation in the first place.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.530
But there was something coming.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:27.130
A prediction that SCP-001 refused to speak
out loud under any circumstances.

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:32.279
It had just one request that it would repeat
to every interviewer for decades after decade:

00:11:32.279 --> 00:11:36.270
they had to free it so that it could prevent
this event from happening.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.050
It must be allowed to construct a device;
otherwise...

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.920
well, it never actually explains that part.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:46.740
It neither does it explain what this device
is and point blank refuses to allow another

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.029
to build this device in its stead.

00:11:49.029 --> 00:11:53.829
Due to the unpredictability of what would
happen if SCP-001 was allowed to construct

00:11:53.829 --> 00:11:59.210
this device, the Overseer Council has point-blank
refused to entertain the notion.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:02.170
But Andrea just couldn't shake it from her
mind.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:07.800
This SCP was capable of accurately and precisely
predicting all number of future events, right

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:12.380
down to the granular details, no matter how
tragic and how dark.

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:15.750
Yet, there was an unknown in its future.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:20.970
It didn't know whether it would be released
or not; it had to beg for its freedom.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:26.610
Whatever it needed this device to defeat was
clearly so powerful that it went beyond SCP-001's

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.139
capacity to predict and understand it.

00:12:29.139 --> 00:12:35.699
There was an event coming that was so significant
that it created chaos and uncertainty in SCP-001's

00:12:35.699 --> 00:12:37.579
perfect view of the future.

00:12:37.579 --> 00:12:42.519
And there was only one entity that Andrea
trusted to be powerful enough to counteract

00:12:42.519 --> 00:12:43.620
this event.

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:47.950
And as she lay there dying in a pool of her
own blood, she looked up through the glass

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:53.590
window in the door at the entity she trusted
and watched as it keyed in the passcode to

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:54.590
the door.

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:58.550
The door slid open, and the SCP took off running
along the hallway.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:03.329
With her dying breaths, Andrea told the SCP
the passcode to the door that it had just

00:13:03.329 --> 00:13:06.690
opened, understanding the brilliance of what
had just happened.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:12.110
The SCP had been able to open that door because
it had seen the future where she told it the

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:13.209
password.

00:13:13.209 --> 00:13:17.600
And with that, she smiled and laid her head
down on the hard floor.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:18.600
*

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:20.880
Except SCP-001 did not manage to escape.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.970
It was gunned down in the hallway with 247
bullets that brutalized its body and wheeled

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:32.510
back into its containment cell, where it began
the slow process of healing.

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:37.060
For most of the research team, things went
back to business as usual, albeit with slightly

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:38.640
better security.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:42.560
But to the handful of researchers who really
thought about what had happened, a sense of

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:45.240
unease settled on their shoulders.

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:50.380
SCP-001 would have known before it ran out
of that cell and attempted Andrea's plan that

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:52.540
its escape was going to fail.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:57.620
It would have known decades prior the exact
positions of the 247 bullets that pounded

00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:58.620
into its body.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:03.709
It would have known the searing agony that
it was to endure for months as its body slowly

00:14:03.709 --> 00:14:06.440
healed from its failed escape attempt.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:09.640
And yet, it had gone through with it anyway.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.860
What set of events had been put into motion
when Andrea carved those three letters into

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:15.310
the plastic tray?

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:19.380
What greater purpose was SCP-001 working towards?

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:22.220
And was it now too late to stop it?

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:29.390
Now check out “SCP-001 - 15 Files Protected
By A Memetic Kill Agent!” and “SCP-001

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:30.529
- YOU Are The Anomaly!”

